TY  - JOUR
T1  - Farewell to journalism? Time for a rethinking.
AN  - 1312413445; 0001182927
AB  - This article argues that journalism is in freefall collapse in the US and that it can only be halted by massive public subsidy. It outlines the key features of what a healthy journalism is and uses public good theory to show how it might be achieved. There are many indicators that journalism in the US today, particularly political journalism, is unhealthy: editorial staffs have shrunk, the number of newspapers and newsrooms has declined sharply, and while there is no shortage of "news", a very high proportion of it is generated by ever fewer sources and much of the information originates as government or corporate spin. In 1960 there was less than one PR agent for every working journalist, a ratio of 0.75-to-1; by 1990 the ratio was over 2-to-1; in 2012 it was 4-to-1. At the same time as the number of journalists has declined, the wages of those in employment have been slashed while their workloads have increased: in an era of ever-increasing corruption, the watchdog is no longer on the beat. While old media downsizes, new media are not even beginning to fill the void; as traditional journalism disintegrates, no models have been found to make Web journalism profitable. This is a failure of the market that can only be rectified by public subsidy. Those that categorically reject public subsidies, fearing government control over the content of journalism, actually misunderstand how it works and misrepresent its history: press subsidies are as American as apple pie; the democratic culture of the US was built on them. Public good theory helps explain this, and why the market fails journalism. Two recent research projects (by "The Economist" and Freedom House) found that the nations with the strongest democracies were those with the freest press systems. They are the same nations that make the greatest public investment in journalism. (Quotes from original text)
