TY  - JOUR
T1  - Visioning the Sustainable City
AN  - 61703083; 200813164
AB  - This contribution to Part 2 of a UK Symposium, in light of the rapid rate of urbanization, seeks to envision a sustainable city. Even such a short time ago as the late 1950s, 75% of the world's population lived in rural areas. Now the majority live in urban areas, & the rate is increasing. In the poorer cities, a billion people live without adequate homes & services; while in the more affluent cities, the people gobble up natural resources from around the globe. Many ecologists have responded with an anti-city outlook, but this article considers whether "urbanized" really means "unsustainable." In visioning the sustainable city, the authors make a distinction between urbanization & the ethos of the city. While cities have been known for their corruption & decadence, they have also been centers of human development & innovation. These elements can be brought to bear on the challenge of making cities sustainable by taking a green perspective. The very fact that cities minimize the distance travelled for human interaction is already to the good. This good could be maximized by the plan of some radical city planners to achieve a compact city -- a walking city with adequate & beautiful open & green space instead of streets crowded with cars & trucks. It is contended, however, that sustainable cities could never be built on present patterns of neoliberal capitalism, which privatization & undermines public expenditure. S. Stanton
