TY  - JOUR
T1  - The need for social welfare in sub-Saharan Africa
AN  - 58739645; 2006-1205970
AB  - This study examines the generic problems currently associated with the effective provision of social welfare services in most of the sub-Saharan African countries. There is very little or no poverty alleviation programs in the form of income transfer subsidies, farm subsidies, pension subsidies, unemployment and disability benefits, housing, health care - targeted to the poorest segments of the society. This is happening in the face of increasing misery, due to high population growth rate, worsening economic conditions, famine, conflicts, shortage of land, and the devas­tating impact of HIV/AIDS on the population. The scant existing programs are only accessible to the privileged few. Public spending on human development and social services as a percentage of GDP is very miniscule. Persistent resource constraints are often blamed as the reason why social welfare programs and services can not be provided to a broad majority of the population. This situation is not the case in governments' military expenditures, as well as expenditures on other projects that have little relevance to the pressing unmet needs of the citizens in the region. So much of the limited public resources are also wasted on corruption and mismanagement, fueled by the poorly functioning bureaucracies and weak public administration that does not hold public officials accountable for their malfeasance.
