TY  - JOUR
T1  - The Singapore Developmental State in the New Economy and Polity
AN  - 60607178; 200208604
AB  - Good corporate governance has proved lacking in other Asian countries since the crisis in 1997. Economic reform, corporate & financial restructuring have been urged, cajoled & demanded by major international lenders & probably, too, to avert the next financial crisis. Singapore stands out without the profligacy of government spending, dubious government-business relations, cronyism, corruption, & nepotism. Its formula of a committed developmental state & Singapore Inc has delivered jobs, income, homes, education, security & welfare since 1959. This paper puts to question the resilience of this developmental state model in the globalized environment with information, communication technology (ICT), knowledge-based economy (KBE), & hyper-competition. Efficiency, effectiveness, & deliverables may remain assured under Singapore Inc, which is being restructured even before 1997. The critical question is on the other side of the political economy. The same new economy trends impact on sociopolitical values, culture, & behavior as well. Can Singapore Inc reinvent itself economically without concomitant political reforms & still meet the challenges of growing affluence, new political culture, democratization & impact of ICT on the society & polity? How much to deregulate politically to match economic deregulation & liberalization? How much more corporate governance & transparency in Singapore Inc? Foreign economic policy has to be more sensitive when government-linked companies (GLCs) cross borders & interact in the political economy & social milieu in the region. The paper advances a number of scenarios for the Singapore developmental state & a "flow-through five-star Hotel Singapore" with a loyal clientele seems a likely one. 4 Tables, 83 References. Adapted from the source document.
