TY  - JOUR
T1  - Corruption and the composition of government expenditure
AN  - 203910703; 01665952
AB  - This study asks whether predatory behavior by corrupt politicians distorts the composition of government expenditure.  Corruption is found to reduce government spending on education in a cross section of countries.  A possible interpretation of the observed correlation between corruption and government expenditure composition is that corrupt governments find it easier to collect bribes on some expenditure items than on others.  Education stands out as a particularly unattractive target for rent-seekers, presumably in large part because its provision typically does not require high-technology inputs to be provided by oligopolistic suppliers.
