TY  - JOUR
T1  - The cost of bribery and self-dealing in the S&L failures
AN  - 201015111; 01055622
AB  - The long-term objective of a study was to assess the importance in the economy of bribery and self-dealing.  The study was not easy because much of the data were hidden.  Estimates of the actual value to the public of government spending lead only to ideological wrangling.  But in the 1980s, a wave of savings & loans (S&L) failures provided unique new material for study.  Because these were financial institutions, their losses were in dollars, which makes unequivocal analysis possible.  And the lost money did not come from nebulous sources - it was and is being born directly by taxpayers.  The end results reported are the cost-bribe and cost-loot ratios for the S&L failures.
