TY  - JOUR
T1  - Inefficiencies in water project design and operation in the Third World: an economic perspective
AN  - 13689750; 199400023
AB  - Water projects in developing countries are frequently poorly operated and maintained, resulting in benefits falling short of plans. The problems begin in the identification, design and construction stages: the attitudes of both donor and host countries result in inappropriate projects, unsustainable technologies and a poor quality of construction. Consequently, operation and maintenance are difficult or impossible. Donor faults include the desire to build over-elaborate plants, sell technology, and provide excessive capital to favoured organizations. The host country is responsible for excessive administrative centralization, lack of rewards for good operation and maintenance, and widespread corruption. Until these deficiencies are addressed, Third World water projects will continue to have low or negative net returns.
