TY  - JOUR
T1  - How Corruption May Corrupt
AN  - 230835547; 00486026
AB  - The incidence of corruption seems to vary strongly across societies, even for comparable activities.  A model is presented to highlight how the profitability of bureaucratic corruption may be related to its frequency.  Several mechanisms can be used in explaining the stylized facts of varying incidence of corruption.  There might be multiple self-fulfilling equilibria levels of corruption even when both supply and demand of corrupt acts are considered.  Members of a public organization act in a corrupt way if they directly or indirectly deal with a nonmember and use the organization's resources, including their decision-making power and special information, to acquire payment that is against the rules of the organization or against the law.  In countries with much political corruption, a cut in public spending may actually reduce the overall levels of corruption.
